Patch: trimAllocs
Letter: H
Requires: none
Files: src/datetime.js, src/duration.js
Summary: three objects built to be read once and dropped: clone's second config, as()'s intermediate Duration, endOf's per-call unit literal

H: three objects that exist only to be read once

Same shape as G's third family, and a separate patch only because it is a
separate argument: G's two are on the arithmetic *math*, where the risk is
floating point, and these three are on the arithmetic *plumbing*, where the risk
is that some caller depends on a property of the object being removed. Each of
the three is a few lines. Together they are worth more than any of them, because
two of the three are under every setter DateTime has.

  datetime.js  clone   builds its config twice. `current` is the seven fields
                       being carried over, and then `{ ...current, ...alts,
                       old: current }` is a second object built by merging it
                       with the caller's changes. Naming the seven fields costs
                       one comparison each and removes the merge.
  duration.js  as      is shiftTo(unit).get(unit): a whole Duration constructed,
                       normalized and cloned again on the way out of shiftTo, so
                       that one number can be read off it and the rest thrown
                       away.
  datetime.js  endOf   builds `{ [unit]: 1 }` per call. A computed key makes a
                       dictionary-mode object, which normalizeObject then walks.
                       There are nine of them and they never change.

clone is the one that matters, and not only for the allocation. The second object
takes its shape from `alts`, which is a different set of keys at each of the six
call sites — {ts}, {ts, zone, wasHole}, {loc}, {ts, o, wasHole}, and adjustTime's
{ts, o} — so the config the DateTime constructor reads is a different shape on
every path into it, and the constructor's field reads never settle on one. Naming
the fields gives every call site the same shape.

Two of the seven fields turn out to be dead in the merged object, which is worth
knowing before reading the comparisons as defensive: the constructor ignores
`config.o` whenever `config.old` is set, which clone always sets, and no call site
passes `invalid` at all. They are forwarded anyway, so that this is a rewrite of
the spread and not a bet about which of its fields anyone needs.

as() is the fiddly one, because shiftTo is not the plain sum it reduces to. Two
things are copied out of it rather than simplified away:

The trunc-and-remainder split. shiftTo takes the integer part of the running
total and carries `(own * 1000 - whole * 1000) / 1000` as the remainder, then
adds it back. That is not the identity in floating point, and `whole + remainder`
is the number callers have been getting.

The `|| 0`. get(unit) reads through the unit getter, which ends in `|| 0`, so a
Duration whose value for that unit is NaN answers 0 today. Duration.fromMillis
throws on NaN before it can reach here, but fromDurationLike and the matrix
divisions can still produce one, and the difference would only show up as a 0
turning into a NaN somewhere downstream.

And one thing that had to be found rather than reasoned about: as("__proto__")
does not throw. Duration.normalizeUnit looks its argument up in an object
literal, where "__proto__" finds Object.prototype and "constructor" finds a
function, and both are truthy enough to pass the check that is supposed to reject
them. shiftTo then matches no unit at all and the getter reads undefined off what
comes back. The sum here has no such fixed point — it would answer 0, or throw
out of the matrix — so anything normalizeUnit answers that is not one of the nine
ordered units falls back to shiftTo. Correcting normalizeUnit instead is the
better fix and a different patch.

endOf needs a Map and not an object for the memo, because the key is whatever
string the caller passed. `oneOf["__proto__"]` on a plain object finds
Object.prototype, which is truthy and would be handed to plus() as a duration —
so endOf("__proto__"), which throws InvalidUnitError today, would instead
silently behave like startOf. A Map has no such key.

Measured on the all-8 build, fastest of nine interleaved passes, node then bun,
each edit alone and then together:

  clone      plus -15% / -25%, set -13% / -24%, hasSame -10% / -23%,
             endOf -8% / -23%, startOf -9% / -18%, toRelative -5% / -13%
  as         Duration#as -70% / -77%, everything else flat
  endOf      endOf -11% / 0%
  all three  plus -21% / -24%, endOf -20% / -19%, set -14% / -24%,
             startOf -12% / -16%, Duration#as -68% / -75%, toRelative -4% / -17%

with toFormat flat throughout as a control. The engine split runs the way it does
for G's constants: V8 escape-analyzes some of these allocations away and JSC does
not, so bun sees roughly double what node does on clone. The endOf memo is the
exception and the weakest of the three — it is worth something on V8 and nothing
on JSC — and it is here because it is four lines and the other two carry the
patch.

Verification is in benchmarks/test/trim-allocs-patch.test.ts: every one of clone's
call sites across five zones and both DST directions, every unit and its aliases,
the strings that are not units, and Duration#as over fractional, negative,
past-2^53 and non-finite inputs at both conversion accuracies, all against stock.
It compares wasHole and locale as well as the instant, because clone carries
fields that no timestamp reflects, and Object.is rather than === so that NaN and
-0 have to agree. Twelve deliberate breakages of this patch were run against it
and eleven failed the suite; the survivor replaces Math.trunc with Math.round,
which the remainder line then cancels out. Luxon's own suite was also run against
a tree with this applied and produced an identical pass/fail set.

diff --git a/src/datetime.js b/src/datetime.js
--- a/src/datetime.js
+++ b/src/datetime.js
@@ -53,6 +53,22 @@ import Invalid from "./impl/invalid.js";
 
 const INVALID = "Invalid DateTime";
 const MAX_DATE = 8.64e15;
+
+// UPSTREAM-H: endOf built `{ [unit]: 1 }` per call, and there are nine of them.
+// A Map rather than an object because the key is whatever the caller passed:
+// oneOf["__proto__"] on an object finds Object.prototype, which is truthy, and
+// endOf("__proto__") would then behave like startOf instead of throwing.
+const oneOfCache = new Map();
+
+function oneOf(unit) {
+  let one = oneOfCache.get(unit);
+
+  if (one === undefined) {
+    oneOfCache.set(unit, (one = { [unit]: 1 }));
+  }
+
+  return one;
+}
 
 function unsupportedZone(zone) {
   return new Invalid("unsupported zone", `the zone "${zone.name}" is not supported`);
@@ -97,7 +113,23 @@ function clone(inst, alts) {
     invalid: inst.invalid,
     wasHole: inst.wasHole,
   };
-  return new DateTime({ ...current, ...alts, old: current });
+  // UPSTREAM-H: was `new DateTime({ ...current, ...alts, old: current })`, a
+  // second object merged out of the first. `alts` is a different set of keys at
+  // each call site, so the config the constructor reads was a different shape on
+  // every path into it; naming the fields gives it one. `o` and `invalid` are
+  // forwarded for faithfulness rather than for effect — the constructor ignores
+  // `o` whenever `old` is set, which is always here, and nothing passes
+  // `invalid`.
+  return new DateTime({
+    ts: alts.ts === undefined ? current.ts : alts.ts,
+    zone: alts.zone === undefined ? current.zone : alts.zone,
+    c: alts.c === undefined ? current.c : alts.c,
+    o: alts.o === undefined ? current.o : alts.o,
+    loc: alts.loc === undefined ? current.loc : alts.loc,
+    invalid: alts.invalid === undefined ? current.invalid : alts.invalid,
+    wasHole: alts.wasHole === undefined ? current.wasHole : alts.wasHole,
+    old: current,
+  });
 }
 
 // find the right offset a given local time. The o input is our guess, which determines which
@@ -1807,7 +1839,8 @@ export default class DateTime {
    */
   endOf(unit, opts) {
+    // UPSTREAM-H: was this.plus({ [unit]: 1 }); see oneOf() at the top of the file
     return this.isValid
-      ? this.plus({ [unit]: 1 })
+      ? this.plus(oneOf(unit))
           .startOf(unit, opts)
           .minus(1)
       : this;
diff --git a/src/duration.js b/src/duration.js
--- a/src/duration.js
+++ b/src/duration.js
@@ -756,8 +756,52 @@ export default class Duration {
    * @example Duration.fromObject({hours: 60}).as('days') //=> 2.5
    * @return {number}
    */
   as(unit) {
-    return this.isValid ? this.shiftTo(unit).get(unit) : NaN;
+    if (!this.isValid) return NaN;
+
+    // UPSTREAM-H: was this.shiftTo(unit).get(unit) — a Duration built, walked,
+    // normalized and cloned so that one number could be read back off it. With a
+    // single target unit shiftTo's loop is the sum below: everything ordered
+    // above the target converted up into it, everything below divided into it.
+    //
+    // Two things are copied out of shiftTo rather than simplified away. Its
+    // trunc-and-remainder split is not the identity in floating point, and
+    // whole + remainder is the number callers get today. And get() reads through
+    // the unit getter, which ends in `|| 0`, so a NaN answers 0 today.
+    const u = Duration.normalizeUnit(unit);
+    const at = orderedUnits.indexOf(u);
+
+    // normalizeUnit does not always answer with a unit: its table is an object
+    // literal, so "__proto__" resolves to Object.prototype and "constructor" to
+    // a function, both truthy enough to get past its own check. shiftTo then
+    // matches no unit and returns something the getter reads as undefined. That
+    // is luxon's answer today and this is not the patch that changes it.
+    if (at < 0) return this.shiftTo(unit).get(unit);
+
+    const vals = this.values;
+    const matrix = this.matrix;
+
+    let own = 0;
+
+    for (let i = 0; i < at; i++) {
+      const higher = orderedUnits[i];
+      if (isNumber(vals[higher])) own += matrix[higher][u] * vals[higher];
+    }
+
+    if (isNumber(vals[u])) own += vals[u];
+
+    const whole = Math.trunc(own);
+    const rest = (own * 1000 - whole * 1000) / 1000;
+    let out = whole;
+
+    if (rest !== 0) out += rest;
+
+    for (let i = at + 1; i < orderedUnits.length; i++) {
+      const lower = orderedUnits[i];
+      if (isNumber(vals[lower]) && vals[lower] !== 0) out += vals[lower] / matrix[u][lower];
+    }
+
+    return out || 0;
   }
 
